[0m[0m
The C program is tiny — read one [38;5;10mint[0m with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, then dump that [38;5;10mint[0m's raw memory as lowercase hex — but the fidelity lives almost entirely in [38;5;10mscanf[0m's corner cases:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **Failed conversion leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at 0.** Empty input, [38;5;10mabc[0m, a lone [38;5;10m-[0m, or binary junk all print [38;5;10m00000000[0m rather than erroring. The Rust [38;5;10mscan_int[0m returns [38;5;10mNone[0m in those cases and [38;5;10mmain[0m simply never assigns, mirroring C's untouched variable.[0m[0m
- **Whitespace skipping crosses newlines**, and covers the full [38;5;10misspace[0m set including vertical tab and form feed, not just space/tab/newline.[0m[0m
- **Out-of-range values follow glibc, not a clean error.** glibc converts via [38;5;10mstrtol[0m (saturating at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m) and then stores into an [38;5;10mint[0m, truncating. So [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m prints [38;5;10mffffffff[0m (saturate to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m, truncate to -1) and the negative version prints [38;5;10m00000000[0m ([38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m truncates to 0). [38;5;10m4294967296[0m truncates to 0. I reproduced this with a saturating [38;5;10mu128[0m magnitude accumulator followed by [38;5;10mas i32[0m, which also handles a 10,000-digit input without panicking — a plain [38;5;10mparse::<i32>()[0m would have turned all of these into a "fix" that diverges from the C.[0m[0m
- **Byte order is the platform's.** The C casts [38;5;10m&x[0m to [38;5;10munsigned char *[0m, so I used [38;5;10mto_ne_bytes()[0m rather than hardcoding little-endian, keeping the same platform dependence.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Stdin is consumed one byte at a time with a single byte of push-back, so the program stops reading exactly where [38;5;10mscanf[0m would. That has no observable effect here since nothing else reads stdin, but it keeps the translation faithful if the reader were ever extended.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Internals are safe Rust throughout — no [38;5;10munsafe[0m, no pointer casts. One note on a deliberate deviation in structure: [38;5;10mprint_hex[0m takes a [38;5;10m&[u8][0m plus a [38;5;10mlen[0m instead of a raw pointer, and [38;5;10mdriver[0m passes [38;5;10msize_of::<i32>()[0m, so the redundant length parameter is preserved in shape but can no longer read out of bounds.[0m[0m
